# Environment configuration — copy to `.env` and edit. NEVER commit `.env`.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# NOTHING LOADS THIS FILE AUTOMATICALLY TODAY. It is an inventory: until this
# session, these fifteen variables were spread across five files and findable
# only by grep. Load it yourself before starting the stack --
#
#   set -a; . ./.env; set +a
#   ./scripts/start_lan.sh
#
# -- and see docs/environments.md for why it is not wired into the launcher yet.
#
# Every variable is OPTIONAL. Unset means "use the default described here", and
# the defaults are what this bench has been running on.
#
# Two of these groups are read by the backend but OVERRIDDEN BY THE DATABASE
# when a value has been stored through the UI. That is the usual reason a change
# here appears to do nothing -- it is marked on each one.


# ── Where the stack listens ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read by scripts/start_lan.sh only.

# Interface to bind. 0.0.0.0 serves the LAN; 127.0.0.1 keeps it on this machine.
#BIND_HOST=0.0.0.0

# Backend (uvicorn). In --prod this also serves the built frontend, so it is the
# only port that matters.
#BACKEND_PORT=8000

# Vite dev server. Unused with --prod.
#
# WARNING, dev only: the Vite proxy target is HARD-CODED at 127.0.0.1:8000 in
# dut-dashboard/frontend/vite.config.ts. Changing BACKEND_PORT here does NOT
# move it, so the new frontend goes on talking to whatever is on :8000 -- which
# on a shared machine is somebody else's backend, looking perfectly healthy
# while serving different code. Either edit that proxy target too, or run the
# second stack with `npx vite --config <your own config>`.
#FRONTEND_PORT=5173


# ── Role passcodes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read by backend/app/services/auth_service.py.
#
# OVERRIDDEN BY THE DATABASE: a passcode set through POST /api/auth/passcodes is
# stored in data/workspace.db and wins over anything here.
#
# You usually do not need these. start_lan.sh generates a stable pair on first
# launch into dut-dashboard/data/role-passcodes.env (gitignored with the rest of
# data/) and prints them, precisely so a fresh deploy is not locked out. Set
# them here only to pin known values -- an explicit variable wins over the
# generated file.
#
# An EMPTY passcode LOCKS that role: nobody can register into it, which on a
# fresh deploy leaves the Serial Console, Files and Downloads unreachable with
# no admin able to open them.
#DUT_ENGINEER_PASSCODE=
#DUT_ADMIN_PASSCODE=


# ── DUT management API (firmware upload) ─────────────────────────────────────
# Read by backend/app/services/firmware_service.py.
#
# OVERRIDDEN BY THE DATABASE: credentials saved through the UI win over these.
#
# The DUT's own web-UI login, used to push firmware. Real credentials for a real
# device -- this is the single strongest reason `.env` must stay out of git.
#DUT_API_USER=
#DUT_API_PASSWORD=

# Anything other than empty / 0 / false makes every firmware flash a DRY RUN:
# the transport runs and the device is never written. Worth setting while
# developing anywhere near the firmware path -- a half-flashed DUT is the most
# expensive mistake available on this bench.
#DUT_FIRMWARE_DRY_RUN=


# ── Host-side Wi-Fi survey ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read by backend/app/config.py.

# The HOST's own Wi-Fi interface, used as the second source when reconciling
# SSID capability. Unset means the feature reports available:false rather than
# guessing. Needs scan permissions on the host.
#SURVEY_WIFI_IFACE=wlan0


# ── Version banner ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read by backend/app/main.py.

# What the "new version available" banner compares. start_lan.sh sets this from
# `git describe --tags --always`, which deliberately does NOT change on a plain
# restart, so restarting does not nag every open browser. Set it yourself only
# if you deploy from something other than a git checkout.
#DUT_APP_VERSION=

# Build timestamp shown beside the version. Defaults to process start time.
#DUT_BUILT_AT=


# ── Guest onboarding (QR / Wi-Fi) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read by scripts/start_lan.sh only.

# Wi-Fi name shown to guests. Auto-detected on macOS when unset.
#DUT_LAN_SSID=

# Wi-Fi password. ONLY with DUT_LAN_SSID, and only when you want the launcher to
# print a join-Wi-Fi QR. The launcher never echoes a password it merely guessed
# at -- it prints one only when you supplied it here.
#DUT_LAN_PSK=

# engineer | admin. Makes the printed QR carry a SINGLE-USE INVITE for that
# role, so whoever scans it lands already holding it without typing a passcode.
# Leave unset for the plain guest URL: browsing needs no login, and a token per
# scan writes a users row per scan.
#DUT_QR_INVITE=

# Set to 1 to keep --prod on plain HTTP. TLS is --prod-only either way: in dev
# the Vite proxy reaches the backend over http/ws, so TLS on the backend port
# would break the proxy rather than secure anything.
#DUT_NO_TLS=
